00:04:00.735 --> 00:04:02.415
He has a chapter for each rule.

00:04:02.805 --> 00:04:07.755
And so instead you have these
themes that sort of pervade all of

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.750
these chapters, and that is this
sense of sort of a dark world.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.919
And the only way we can survive is
if we maintain certain structures, he

00:04:16.919 --> 00:04:23.430
seems to favor a patriarchy because
historically, and perhaps in the animal

00:04:23.435 --> 00:04:26.310
kingdom, the male is typically stronger.

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:29.250
Uh, and, uh, that's a good thing.

00:04:29.255 --> 00:04:32.909
And, uh, where there is, and,
and all a while, of course, he's

00:04:32.909 --> 00:04:37.210
pushing back against this sort of
woke thing, which makes any sort

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.930
of expression of power a bad thing.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:44.550
Whereas in fact, to a large extent,
people who have more power, but

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:48.870
not always, but in many cases, they
have earned that position of power

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.970
because they have certain abilities.

00:04:50.975 --> 00:04:55.710
And so he stresses this, that, uh,
we can't just condemn the patriarchy,

00:04:56.010 --> 00:05:01.350
but by the same token countries that
are where the patriarchal sort of

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:07.110
pattern is stronger, aren't necessarily
