00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.610
However, where I have trouble with,
with Jordan Peterson is he calls this

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.790
Western society, Western culture.

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:42.360
But, but the fact is that it's modern
society, that Western society, 500

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:47.400
years ago, a thousand years ago, over
2000 years ago in Rome and Greece,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.270
where half the population were slaves.

00:06:49.785 --> 00:06:54.105
Uh, in, in the middle ages when people
were persecuted for what they thought,

00:06:54.105 --> 00:07:00.795
for their religions, uh, not that great
at a time, in many cases throughout

00:07:00.795 --> 00:07:07.405
history where other civilizations it
might be, you know, the, the Chinese,

00:07:07.935 --> 00:07:14.095
uh, civilization during the, uh, the
Tang Dynasty, for example, maybe ... was

00:07:14.095 --> 00:07:16.335
a nicer place to live than London.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.870
uh, you know, I get my dates...

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:23.370
but certainly six, you know, middle
ages in, uh, Europe wasn't all

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.640
that great in terms of lifespan,
in terms of the sophistication, in

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.740
terms of respect for individuals.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:34.860
And so and so on, uh, in the modern age,
uh, county, like Japan has a legal system,

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:41.970
uh, which is, uh, certainly no worse than,
uh, in Western Europe or north America.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.330
It certainly has health
outcomes that are better.

00:07:44.849 --> 00:07:47.580
Uh, it, uh, functions very
effectively as a society.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:51.840
So I, I think this idea sort of to
stick up for Western society and we're

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:57.000
the best and stuff, I find that a
little bit inaccurate, uh, to say the

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:02.609
least, and even to the extent of the
sort of technological achievements in

00:08:02.609 --> 00:08:07.890
Western society, they are the result
of technological advances in other

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.590
countries, mathematics in India and, and.

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.979
Uh, science in China 2000 years ago.

00:08:13.979 --> 00:08:21.060
And, uh, even, uh, you know, Arab
and, and Muslim scholars who sort of,

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:26.430
uh, kept greek learning and Indian
learning and, and Islamic learning,

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:31.020
and eventually brought that back to
the west so that in say the 12, 13th,

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:35.430
14th century, many of these things
were rediscovered and this then led

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:37.419
to the, uh, Renaissance and so forth.

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:39.870
And so on the world is
an interconnected place.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:46.080
And I think, you know, to use his, uh,
imagery, you know, to stand up on your

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.000
hin legs and say ours is the best.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:49.500
Uh, I don't think he's very helpful.

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:54.435
I think if he just, as I'm now learning
so much more about Iran and Persian

00:08:54.435 --> 00:09:00.735
history and, and the, the, uh, Cyrus' the
cylinder where he recognized the freedom

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:05.235
of religion and freedom of, of different
ethnic groups, groups to have every right

00:09:05.235 --> 00:09:07.485
in the, in the old, uh, Persian empire.

00:09:07.755 --> 00:09:12.225
It, it, it kind of enables you to see
the world through different lenses,

00:09:12.225 --> 00:09:13.965
the lenses of different languages.

00:09:14.355 --> 00:09:19.155
And, uh, and I think that gives you
a better perspective, uh, and...

00:09:20.355 --> 00:09:23.685
but I do agree that, um,
that one shouldn't be overly

00:09:23.685 --> 00:09:26.324
critical of, of our society.

00:09:26.475 --> 00:09:30.135
You know, there's a lot of this sort of
anti-capitalist society, but I'm always

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:34.515
aware of the fact that the products that
I use, this camera that I'm using, which

00:09:34.515 --> 00:09:37.965
is made in Japan, or maybe it's made in
China under licensed from the Japanese,

00:09:38.324 --> 00:09:43.965
uh, the food I eat the products I use
the, the daily functioning of our society.

00:09:44.205 --> 00:09:50.099
It's, you know, all kinds of people
making my life richer and better.

00:09:50.099 --> 00:09:53.069
That's how our societies have evolved.

00:09:53.219 --> 00:09:55.170
Of course it ends at some point.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.500
Uh, I don't think that
people are all out to get me.

00:09:58.709 --> 00:10:03.060
I don't think there's so many evil people
out there, but there are evil people.

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:07.380
And so in terms of how we lead
our lives, uh, I don't think we

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:09.420
should say it's all the individual.

00:10:09.449 --> 00:10:10.949
It's not all the individual.

00:10:11.099 --> 00:10:16.425
I think we need to obviously live
our lives in a way, you know, we are

00:10:16.425 --> 00:10:21.015
responsible for our lives, but we
are also helpful to others, which is

00:10:21.015 --> 00:10:24.435
in a way in his book, but it's sort
of hidden in amongst all the sort

00:10:24.435 --> 00:10:29.925
of dark mythology and, you know,
biblical references to punishments

00:10:29.925 --> 00:10:33.525
and, uh, and all kinds of dark stuff.

00:10:33.525 --> 00:10:39.285
He creates this sort of sense that
the world, this, uh, chaotic place and

00:10:39.285 --> 00:10:43.305
the it's sort of difficult to extract
yourself from the tragedy and the pain

00:10:43.305 --> 00:10:48.945
of living and to do that, you gotta, you
know, follow these rules, basic self-help

00:10:48.945 --> 00:10:54.045
things there that are universal, like
tell the truth and listen to others.

00:10:54.465 --> 00:10:58.065
And, uh, you know, look after
yourself, be responsible to

00:10:58.065 --> 00:11:00.285
yourself and all this stuff is good.

00:11:00.285 --> 00:11:04.725
And, but, but, um, I think the
main reason why his, his book

00:11:05.445 --> 00:11:07.454
has, um, been so successful.

00:11:07.454 --> 00:11:08.235
I think there are two reasons.

00:11:08.235 --> 00:11:13.905
One is there's quite a breadth of,
of information about psychology and,

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:18.645
and mythology and religions and all
these different ways that people

00:11:18.975 --> 00:11:21.495
try to explain the mystery of life.

00:11:22.045 --> 00:11:26.475
And, and then he extracts out of
that, a few rules he claims that can

00:11:26.805 --> 00:11:33.495
help mitigate this, this, uh, chaos
and enable you to live a meaningful.

00:11:34.439 --> 00:11:37.080
But where that meaning is you
have to find for yourself.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:40.800
I think obviously our families
provide meaning, but there are

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.210
also dysfunctional families.

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:47.370
Uh, for me, um, learning languages
provide meaning because I discover more

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:52.710
about different cultures, uh, people
who are bird Watchers, uh, people

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:54.480
who are, uh, involved in charities.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.300
There was a gentleman, uh,
discovered the other day, who...

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:05.805
he has set up his whole house as a, as
a, as, you know, uh, recycling Depot and

00:12:05.805 --> 00:12:09.405
you can take your empty milk bottles,
your empty, you know, water bottles,

00:12:09.405 --> 00:12:13.155
your empty wine bottles, take 'em up
there, drop them there and he will make

00:12:13.155 --> 00:12:15.735
sure that that is converted into...

00:12:15.795 --> 00:12:18.165
because the recycling
system pays you for them.

00:12:19.215 --> 00:12:22.065
And from that, he gives food
vouchers to homeless people.

00:12:22.305 --> 00:12:23.565
That's his life, that's meaning.

00:12:23.715 --> 00:12:24.225
He has...

00:12:24.225 --> 00:12:24.975
that's his meaning.

00:12:25.155 --> 00:12:25.725
Wonderful.

00:12:27.855 --> 00:12:32.685
So we find meaning wherever it is like
I, uh, and, and I think where I, I

00:12:32.685 --> 00:12:38.295
agree with, with, um, JordanPeterson
is pushback on all this woke stuff

00:12:38.295 --> 00:12:41.564
like this gender neutral pronouns.

00:12:41.564 --> 00:12:47.594
It doesn't matter if, if there is a
non-binary person, a transgendered

00:12:47.594 --> 00:12:52.994
person and I refer to him or her as
him or her, and they say to me, no,

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.824
I'd rather be referred to as this.

00:12:54.885 --> 00:12:55.785
Fine.

00:12:56.354 --> 00:13:00.675
Um, the idea that somehow a
gender neutral pronoun has some

00:13:00.675 --> 00:13:03.275
great significance I don't buy.

00:13:03.275 --> 00:13:08.505
Uh, in Persian and in Turkish,
they have gender neutral pronouns.

00:13:08.895 --> 00:13:13.755
And yet both of those societies are
more patriarchal and less tolerant

00:13:14.085 --> 00:13:19.365
of non-traditional gender roles or
non-traditional sexual orientation.

00:13:19.365 --> 00:13:25.635
So the fact that that, uh, gender neutral
pronoun is used is meaningless and why,

00:13:25.635 --> 00:13:31.025
you know, whatever the number is of these
non-binary people in our society, I don't

00:13:31.185 --> 00:13:38.130
know, 0.01%, 2%, whatever the number is,
why should everyone have to change their,

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:43.170
um, speech patterns to accommodate this?

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:46.920
The main purpose is to demonstrate
how progressive we are.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.320
See, I use my preferred...

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.410
like just by saying my
preferred pronouns are he/him.

00:13:52.725 --> 00:13:54.285
Now I've said I'm progressive.

00:13:54.465 --> 00:13:55.665
It's meaningless actually.

00:13:56.055 --> 00:13:59.655
Uh, it's like in the company
that I ran, we have always

00:13:59.655 --> 00:14:01.005
hired the best person available.

00:14:01.455 --> 00:14:04.935
We've had Japanese people,
Chinese people, Sikhs there.

00:14:04.935 --> 00:14:06.375
We've had Romanians, Russians.

00:14:06.855 --> 00:14:08.715
Um, I don't know Filipinos.

00:14:08.715 --> 00:14:09.735
We've had gay people.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:13.065
We've had everybody, the best
person we can find always, but

00:14:13.065 --> 00:14:18.690
I don't believe in saying I am
a diversity, you know, employer.

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:21.600
I sign on to the diversity statement.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:22.410
I don't need all of that.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.990
That's the sort of virtue signaling.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:29.040
Rather the principle should be you
hire the best person available.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:32.550
And I am responsible to
myself, to my employees.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:38.910
And that's how I, I, so to that,
making these kinds of virtue

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:43.800
signaling statements, Uh, a condition
of employment or, uh, you know,

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:47.880
the whole department's set up to
deal with diversity and diversity

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:49.230
is better than lack of diversity.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:53.520
For example, I don't buy that
because Japan, uh, Korea, Finland,

00:14:53.525 --> 00:14:56.189
they don't have much diversity
and their successful societies.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.110
And you have other societies like Canada.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:02.069
If you go to Toronto, Vancouver, Montreal,
you're gonna find a tremendous diversity

00:15:02.069 --> 00:15:05.220
of religion, race, and ethnicity.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:09.449
But what really matters is
diversity of points of view.

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:14.219
And I don't think that the societies
that are diverse ethnically or in

00:15:14.219 --> 00:15:18.959
terms of gender are necessarily
better, uh, than less diverse.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.530
But I think it's probably a good
idea to have a diversity of views.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:27.300
And in that regard, I think Jordan
Peterson, while I find a lot of his

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:32.579
stuff, I don't necessarily agree with
it's valuable to have people like that who

00:15:32.849 --> 00:15:39.209
are pushing back against this attempted
you know conformity imposition on people.

00:15:39.209 --> 00:15:41.849
You must say this, you must
make a statement on diversity.

00:15:42.119 --> 00:15:45.630
You must, uh, use gender neutral
pronouns and so forth and so on.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:49.589
So with that, I think I've stirred
up hopefully a bit of controversy.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:54.300
And, but I think diversity of views
is important and we should respect

00:15:54.305 --> 00:15:58.229
everyone's, uh, point of view and
be willing to, uh, discuss and

00:15:58.229 --> 00:16:03.209
defend our views and also take into
consideration the, uh, counter argument.

00:16:03.795 --> 00:16:04.484
There you have it.

00:16:04.604 --> 00:16:05.574
Thank you for listening.

00:16:05.824 --> 00:16:06.384
Bye for now.

